<!-- Phase 9 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase9-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A shared checkout method looks clean in Kotlin, but the Swift caller sees unfamiliar wrappers, an unexpected error shape and work that continues after its task ends. The exported API is the product the iOS team receives. Inspect that surface before deciding which bridge makes it easier to own.</p>

<!-- @after Start With the Export Path :: visual -->
### Three strategies, one consumer contract

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Objective-C export</strong><small>Inspect the generated header and its Swift import.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Objective-C plus wrappers/SKIE</strong><small>Review the wrapper contract as well as the framework.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Swift export</strong><small>Evaluate its current Alpha mappings and supported integration.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Consumer proof</strong><small>Compile the intended Swift calls and observe errors, cancellation and ownership.</small></div></div><figcaption>These are explicit export choices, not interchangeable syntax on the same generated API.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Designing a Swift-First Boundary :: animation -->
### Prove cancellation at the operation

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A cancelled Swift task is the start of the investigation</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="task" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Swift task</strong><small data-text="taskV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="bridge" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Selected export/bridge</strong><small data-text="bridgeV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="job" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Kotlin operation</strong><small data-text="jobV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="evidence" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Observed outcome</strong><small data-text="evidenceV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Start",
"caption": "Use a controlled Kotlin operation that is waiting on an observable suspension or request.",
"state": {
"task": "active",
"job": "active"
},
"text": {
"taskV": "awaiting result",
"bridgeV": "chosen path recorded",
"jobV": "waiting",
"evidenceV": "not yet cancelled"
},
"add": [
"task",
"bridge",
"job",
"evidence"
]
},
{
"label": "Cancel task",
"caption": "Cancel the owner on the Swift side.",
"state": {
"task": "warn",
"bridge": "active"
},
"text": {
"taskV": "cancellation requested"
}
},
{
"label": "Check propagation",
"caption": "Determine whether this exact bridge propagates cancellation to the Kotlin job and underlying operation.",
"state": {
"job": "active",
"evidence": "active"
},
"text": {
"evidenceV": "job and request inspected"
}
},
{
"label": "Assert lifetime",
"caption": "Also check terminal errors and released callbacks. Task syntax alone is insufficient evidence.",
"state": {
"evidence": "done"
},
"text": {
"evidenceV": "documented result for this bridge/version"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>The chapter describes different bridge guarantees. Run this probe against the API and artifact your consumers actually use.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Failure Modes to Catch in Review :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>A Kotlin facade compiles. Is Swift compatibility proven?</summary>

No. Inspect generated names and types, then compile a representative Swift consumer. Kotlin source alone cannot reveal every exported mapping.
</details>

<details><summary>Should every implementation class become public to Swift?</summary>

Prefer a small deliberate facade and immutable boundary models. Export only what the consumer needs and govern its error and lifecycle contract.
</details>

<details><summary>SKIE produces typed state sequences. Does the Kotlin model become ObservableObject?</summary>

No. The Swift adapter still owns presentation adaptation and MainActor mutation. The bridge handles its documented cross-language behaviour, not every UI responsibility.
</details>
